  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1026 PM MDT Tue Aug 18 2026\n\nA frontal boundary is dropping south through eastern Utah and \nwestern Colorado tonight into Wednesday, triggering some scattered \nshowers and thunderstorms. An accompanying upper level jet is \nkeeping this shower activity tracking along pretty quickly, and dry \nsurface levels are limiting the amount of precipitation reaching the \nground. The main concerns continue to be gusty outflow winds and \nlightning. Showers and storms will taper off after midnight, but \nwill redevelop across the higher terrain Wednesday afternoon and \nevening, again with the same concerns. The frontal boundary will \ndrift east by Thursday as ridging builds in across the Four Corners. \nEnough moisture trapped under this ridge will keep chances for \nmountain showers and thunderstorms in the forecast, but storm motion \nwill slow right down with the lack of steering flow. This means \nthat, while surface levels remain dry, a slow moving storm over a \nsensitive area could eventually lead to some heavy rain and flash \nflooding concerns. Otherwise, temperatures continue to rise through \nthe end of the work week, running around 10 degrees above normal by \nFriday. Triple digit heat will be possible across the desert valleys \nof eastern Utah and western Colorado.\n\nAn omega block sets up for the weekend, with the high centered just \nfar enough east of the Divide for southwesterly flow to dominate \nover the Great Basin and Southwest. As a result, monsoonal moisture \nwill begin advecting into the region. Discrepancies on things like \ntiming, intensity, and extent of the moisture plume remain between \ndeterministic and ensemble model solutions. However, the overall \nsignal of above normal moisture returning to eastern Utah and \nwestern Colorado remains, increasing forecast confidence. Clouds, \nshowers, and thunderstorms will become more widespread through the \nweekend, along with a return to more seasonable temperatures. \nFurther speculation on impacts will depend on storm motion, the \npotential for training storms, and instability resulting from cloud \ncover (or the lack thereof), all of which are too early to speak on \nwith confidence at this time. We'll have to wait on higher \nresolution guidance and better model consensus before these details \nbecome clearer.",
